# Qeet Developers — local configuration
#
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Copy to `.env.local`, not `.env`: Astro loads `.env` in every mode, including
# `bun run build`, so anything set there silently affects a deployed build.
#
# Only `PUBLIC_`-prefixed variables reach the browser, which these must, since
# the API reference renders client-side.
#
# Nothing here is a secret. Never put API keys in this file — the portal holds
# no credentials; you paste your own into the API client at runtime.
#
# You do not need this file to work on the portal. Defaults are correct:
# `bun install && bun run dev` is the whole setup.


# ─── API client proxy ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ON by default (`/api/proxy`) and you almost certainly want it that way.
#
# No Qeet service sends CORS headers, local ones included — verified:
# http://localhost:4001/healthz answers 200 to curl and "Failed to fetch" to a
# browser on the same machine. Without the proxy, Send cannot work.
#
# The proxy runs server-side, which is why the portal is meant to be run
# locally alongside your stack: they share a machine, so it can reach
# localhost. Its allow-list is derived from `envs()` in
# src/config/products.ts, so it can only ever reach a configured host.
#
# Set it empty to bypass the proxy and call APIs directly. Only useful once a
# service actually sends CORS headers, or to see the raw browser error:
#
#     PUBLIC_QEET_PROXY_URL=
#
# Never point this at a third-party proxy — these requests carry live API keys.
